Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["EPS Axiomatics", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell["\<\
The EPS axiomatisation (Ehlers, Pirani and Schild) builds the geometry of \
spacetime from what observers can do: light rays fix the conformal structure, \
freely falling particles the projective structure, and observers exchanging \
light signals read both off their clocks. On a graph the ingredients are the \
heads of the Causal Infrageometry guide: events are vertices, light rays are \
the members of the light cones (axiom L), freely falling particles are the \
timelike geodesics (axiom P), and an observer is a worldline whose positions \
are read as the ticks of a clock. The compatibility axiom C, that light rays \
are the null geodesics of the projective structure, has no construction on a \
graph yet and is open.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This guide holds the constructions of the axioms D on the observers. Two \
observers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["a", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3460c3ff-fb2a-4767-bc47-f0942a2c8390"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4cb90af9-62e2-4045-9d9f-558618f6fa0a"],
 " exchange light: the message relation sends a tick of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["a", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "80fce923-d7af-4433-b4e7-616f862158af"],
 " to the ticks of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "bc988621-180a-49c8-b50e-85616ebc0056"],
 " it reaches by a light ray, and the echo relation returns the message to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["a", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "91c62d6a-8a6d-4127-b37f-a1bcde2b80e9"],
 ". Axiom D1 asks the message to be a monotone map of the clock of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["a", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ef41cd9d-6446-44c8-bece-82a1066b1797"],
 " into the clock of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b2a4ad93-81a9-4835-b348-05dc8c04ae08"],
 "; on a graph the relation is set-valued and partial, its monotone \
single-valued selections are the message functions, an echo function is the \
composition of a message function to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ed8d254d-942d-4382-aef7-070c913dd525"],
 " with one back to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["a", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "44cd42a4-99af-4032-8221-089435316f49"],
 ", and the axiom holds exactly when the relation is itself such a map. Axiom \
D2 asks the clocks to coordinatise the events: the radar coordinates of an \
observer give every event of its radar domain the emission tick ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["i", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "f9440c5d-6e0c-4545-9ee9-0cc92068b5ca"],
 " of the latest tick that can signal it and the reception tick ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["j", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "fa038379-1b42-41e5-b644-2e898a8283c3"],
 " of the earliest tick it can signal, hence the radar time ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["i", "TI"], "+", 
    StyleBox["j", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "/", "2"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "479ef6fc-9e29-47ca-819f-5b98fa6a17cf"],
 " and the radar distance ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["j", "TI"], "-", 
    StyleBox["i", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "/", "2"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "68c584da-55c0-49e9-ab00-e0c40ef72f70"],
 ", and the axiom holds when the coordinates are injective. Since the \
distance is unsigned, a single observer in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"1", "+", "1"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a63d172d-0adf-4484-9734-eb4e3f25e91d"],
 " dimensions sees the two sides of its worldline folded onto each other; the \
observer space, the radar domain fibered over the coordinate cells, records \
what the observers cannot tell apart, and the defects and the graph k-factor \
measure how far a pair of observers is from the axioms."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

